\cf3 Diffuse disc bulges with minimal thecal sac compression and ligament/facet hypertrophy are common degenerative findings, especially with prior spinal surgery. While not typically an acute emergency, these changes can cause chronic back pain or nerve irritation. Initial management usually involves physical therapy to strengthen core muscles, anti-inflammatory medications, and targeted injections to reduce pain and inflammation. Surgery is generally considered only if conservative measures fail to provide relief or if there are progressive neurological deficits. (Question: Is diffuse bulge with minimal compression of the thecal sac with hypertrophy of the ligamenta flava a serious concern?in my mri diffuse bulge with minimal compression of the thecal sac with hypertrophy of the ligamenta flava and posterior articulating facets in levels L2-3 level,L 3-4, L 4-5 and L1-i have back surgery in 2012 and neck sugery in 2013. what can be done?)\cell \row

