\cf3 Percocet is a common prescription for severe pain after major oral surgery and is generally safe when taken as directed for short-term acute pain relief. It effectively manages post-operative discomfort. Your surgeon will evaluate your pain level, healing progress, and overall condition during your Tuesday appointment. Based on this assessment, they will determine if a further prescription for pain medication, including Percocet, is medically appropriate for your continued recovery. (Question: How safe is taking Percocet for tooth pain after oral surgery?i had major oral surgery on saturday. it was an emergency. a fractured tooth cemented to a bridge was removed, and the tooth on the other side of bridge was almost gone but still cememted with a metal bar wedged in my gum. still in so much pain, he gave me 12 percocets, 10 mgs...will he give me more on tuesday? cant take the pain)\cell \row

