\cf3 A TIA is a warning sign; the goal of treatment is to prevent future strokes, not "cure" the event itself. The risk of future events remains but is significantly reduced with medication. Long-term Levipil can cause dizziness, fatigue, or mood changes. Lobazam may cause drowsiness and can lead to dependence. Statorasp (aspirin/statin) carries risks of bleeding, muscle pain, or liver enzyme elevation. Medication duration is highly individualized, often long-term for stroke prevention (Statorasp). (Question: Suffered from TIA with seizure like symptoms, on Levipil, Lobazam, Statorasp. Will it be cured?my farher (71 years old) recently suffered from TIA with seizure like symptoms. MRI, CT scan and angiogram were carried out and the doctor prescribed Levipil 500mg syrup and tablets, Lobazam 10mg, Statorasp 75/10mg. I would like to know what are the long term side effects. Whether this will be cured without any future attacks and how long the medicine should be continued?)\cell \row

