\cf3 These are likely preauricular pits (or sinuses), a common congenital malformation. They occur when parts of the ear don't fuse completely during fetal development. The initial kidney check was appropriate, as there's a rare association with renal anomalies. While usually harmless, these pits can sometimes collect dead skin cells or fluid, leading to the discharge you observe. The primary concern is infection, which would manifest as redness, swelling, pain, or pus. (Question: What causes tiny holes on outside of both ears?My son has tiny holes on outside of both ears, when he was born, the Dr got an x-ray to make sure his kidneys were fine. Since then no one has said anything about the holes. I asked our pediatrician and she said as long as they don t get infected. Should I be worried, sometimes a substance does come out, but it s not frequent.)\cell \row

