\cf3 A small red spot on your newborn's tongue, present since birth, could certainly be an infantile hemangioma. These common vascular birthmarks often appear shortly after birth and can be quite small. While many hemangiomas grow rapidly before stabilizing and regressing, some remain small or resolve spontaneously without significant growth. Consequences for a small tongue tip lesion are usually benign. Rarely, larger or ulcerated hemangiomas can cause feeding difficulties, pain, or bleeding. Observation by your pediatrician is key. (Question: What causes red spot on tongue of baby?Hi, my newborn has a little red spot on the tip of her tongue, which looks like hemangioma but has not grown so far (she is 1 month old). I noticed it on the second day of her birth. Is it possible that she has hemangioma there and if yes what are the consequences?)\cell \row

