\cf3 Metronidazole is commonly used in lactating mothers. While it passes into breast milk, significant adverse effects in infants are rare. To minimize exposure, take your dose immediately after feeding. Monitor your baby for mild side effects like diarrhea or thrush. Treating your amoebiasis is important. Metronidazole is a highly effective first-line treatment; other alternatives often have similar breastfeeding considerations. (Question: Is it dangerous to breastfeed a baby while on Metronidazole?im a lactating mother to a 6month old child. i have been diagnosed w/ amoebiasis and was prescribed with Metronidazole. My child wont accept bottled milk so i am forced to let her still feed through me. Is it risky for my child? what other medicine can replaced Metronidazole that can be good for my child?)\cell \row

