\cf3 Falling on your buttocks transmits force through the pelvis and spine. Your abdominal pain with movement (getting up, changing positions) and in specific postures strongly suggests a **musculoskeletal injury**. This commonly includes a **strain or contusion of abdominal muscles**, hip flexors, or pelvic floor muscles that were suddenly stretched or contracted. Less frequently, the impact can cause bruising of the pelvic bones or surrounding soft tissues, which can refer pain to the lower abdomen. (Question: What causes abdominal pain after falling on the buttocks?The other day when I went skating my friend pulled me downcand it made me fall right on my butt. Ever since then I have had abdominal pains whenever I get up from lying down or sitting down. I also cannot lay down in certain positions or else I will get sharp pains. Do you know what I could have done? I have no back pains or pains anywhere else except for my abdominal/ torso area.)\cell \row

