\cf3 Your sternum pain, particularly with breathing, strongly suggests costochondritis. This is a common inflammation of the rib cartilage, often triggered by chest surgery, radiation, or muscle strain. While less likely, implant-related irritation could be a factor. Metastasis is a concern for cancer survivors, but musculoskeletal causes are far more frequent. A clinical examination is crucial to determine the exact cause. If it's costochondritis, treatment typically involves anti-inflammatory medications, pain relievers, and heat or ice application to the area. (Question: How to treat the pain caused in the sternum for a person having bilateral mastectomy?I was diagnosed with Stage 2A breast cancer in February 2011. I have had a bilateral mastectomy and chemotherapy and now have saline breast implants. For the past few days, I have been experiencing pain between the 5th and 6th ribs to the left of my sternum. The pain is worse when I breathe. Is this costochondritis? A problem with the implants? Metastasis? Please give me your thoughts. Thank you.)\cell \row

