\cf3 Dietary factors are not typically a primary cause of enuresis, especially after alarms and desmopressin have been tried. While some parents explore avoiding bladder irritants like caffeine or artificial sweeteners, robust scientific evidence linking specific food intolerances to enuresis is limited. Routine food allergy tests (IgE-mediated) are generally unhelpful unless other allergic symptoms are present. "Food intolerance" tests (e.g., IgG) are not medically validated for diagnosing intolerances or guiding treatment for enuresis. (Question: Suggest treatment for enursisI have a son who suffers with enursis. We have used the usual methods of the alarm and desmopressin. It has been suggested to me to look at his diet and foods which he may be intolerant to. Please could you offer some advice on this and am I able to get any food allergy tests carried out anywhere. Thankyou)\cell \row

